        judges(1, 17, "Numbers", 21, 3, "Judah's destruction of Zephath and naming it Hormah recalls Israel's earlier vow and destruction of the same place."),
        judges(1, 18, "Joshua", 11, 22, "The Anakim remaining in Gaza, Gath, and Ashdod explain why Judah's later conquest did not remove them from those Philistine cities."),
        judges(1, 20, "Deuteronomy", 1, 36, "Caleb's possession of Hebron fulfills Moses' promise to him for following the Lord wholeheartedly."),
        judges(1, 20, "Joshua", 15, 13, "Judges confirms Joshua's account that Caleb received Hebron and drove out the sons of Anak."),
        judges(1, 20, "Numbers", 14, 24, "Caleb's inheritance in Hebron is the promised reward for the faithfulness God commended after the spies' rebellion."),
        judges(1, 21, "Joshua", 15, 63, "Both passages record the unresolved Jebusite presence in Jerusalem after the allotment of the land."),
        judges(1, 23, "Genesis", 28, 19, "Bethel's campaign concerns the city Jacob named after his encounter with God there."),
        judges(1, 29, "Joshua", 16, 10, "Judges records the continuing result of Ephraim's earlier failure to drive the Canaanites from Gezer."),
        judges(1, 29, "1 Kings", 9, 16, "Gezer's Canaanite population endured until Pharaoh later captured and burned the city in Solomon's time."),
        judges(1, 31, "Joshua", 19, 24, "The towns whose Canaanite residents Asher left in place lie within the inheritance Joshua assigned to Asher."),
        judges(2, 3, "Numbers", 33, 55, "The angel's warning that remaining Canaanites will become traps applies Moses' earlier warning about leaving the land's inhabitants in place."),
        judges(2, 9, "Joshua", 24, 30, "Judges repeats Joshua's burial at Timnath-heres, the same burial place recorded under its alternate name, Timnath-serah."),
        judges(2, 11, "Judges", 10, 6, "Israel's later service of the Baals repeats the apostasy introduced after the generation that knew Joshua passed away."),
        judges(2, 11, "Judges", 3, 7, "The first oppression cycle begins by describing Israel's forgetfulness and Baal worship in terms that develop this diagnosis."),
        judges(3, 9, "Judges", 1, 13, "Othniel's earlier courage in taking Debir precedes his commissioning as Israel's first deliverer."),
        judges(3, 13, "Judges", 1, 16, "The Kenite descendants who entered Judah with the tribe later settle near Arad, explaining their location in Othniel's story."),
        judges(3, 13, "Deuteronomy", 34, 3, "The city of palms identifies Jericho, where Eglon establishes the Moabite occupation."),
        judges(3, 31, "Judges", 5, 6, "Deborah's song remembers Shamgar as a judge whose time was marked by unsafe roads before the victory over Sisera."),
        judges(4, 2, "Joshua", 11, 1, "Jabin's rule in Hazor continues the history of the northern Canaanite capital Joshua had defeated."),
        judges(4, 6, "Hebrews", 11, 32, "Hebrews names Barak among those whose faith trusted God for deliverance, recalling his role in the victory over Sisera."),
        judges(4, 10, "Judges", 5, 18, "Deborah's song praises Zebulun and Naphtali for risking their lives in the battle Barak led from Kedesh."),
        judges(4, 11, "Numbers", 10, 29, "Heber belongs to the Kenite family descended from Hobab, Moses' Midianite relative and wilderness guide."),
        judges(4, 11, "Judges", 1, 16, "Heber's clan belongs to the Kenites who had settled in Judah's wilderness after entering Canaan with Israel."),
        judges(5, 4, "Deuteronomy", 33, 2, "Deborah's description of the Lord coming from Seir and Sinai echoes Moses' portrayal of the Lord's appearing from Sinai to bless Israel."),
        judges(5, 5, "Exodus", 19, 18, "The trembling mountains in Deborah's song recall Sinai shaking when the Lord descended in fire."),
        judges(5, 6, "Judges", 3, 31, "The song places its account in Shamgar's days, linking the road insecurity to the earlier judge named in the narrative.")
    ]

    private nonisolated static let entriesPart20: [Entry] = [
        judges(5, 18, "Judges", 4, 10, "The song celebrates the costly courage of the two tribes Barak summoned for the battle against Sisera."),
        judges(5, 19, "Judges", 1, 27, "The battle at Taanach occurs in territory where Manasseh had failed to remove Canaanite inhabitants, exposing the unfinished conquest behind the conflict."),
        judges(5, 20, "Judges", 4, 15, "The stars fighting against Sisera poetically interprets the rout the narrative attributes to the Lord's defeat of his army."),
        judges(5, 21, "Judges", 4, 7, "The Kishon sweeping away Sisera's forces fulfills the Lord's promise to draw Sisera to the river for defeat."),
        judges(5, 24, "Judges", 4, 17, "Jael's blessing in the song interprets her decisive hospitality and killing of the fleeing Sisera."),
        judges(6, 3, "Judges", 7, 12, "The Midianite raids introduced here explain the vast Midianite camp Gideon later faces in the valley."),
        judges(6, 24, "Judges", 8, 32, "Gideon's altar marks his encounter with the Lord, while the later notice of his burial closes the story of the judge raised up here."),
