        judges(7, 1, "Judges", 6, 32, "Gideon's camp at the spring follows his receiving the name Jerubbaal after tearing down Baal's altar."),
        judges(7, 1, "Genesis", 12, 6, "Moreh is an established landmark in the land, first named where Abram received God's promise near Shechem."),
        judges(7, 22, "1 Kings", 4, 12, "Abel-meholah later marks part of Solomon's administrative district, preserving the location reached by the fleeing Midianites."),
        judges(7, 25, "Isaiah", 10, 26, "Isaiah recalls the Lord's blow against Midian at the rock of Oreb as a pattern for his promised deliverance."),
        judges(7, 25, "Judges", 8, 3, "The capture of Oreb and Zeeb is the victory Gideon cites when answering Ephraim's complaint."),
        judges(8, 5, "Genesis", 33, 17, "Gideon's pursuit passes through Succoth, the place Jacob named when he settled there after returning to Canaan."),
        judges(8, 33, "Judges", 9, 4, "Gideon's turning to Baal-berith after his death provides the religious setting for Abimelech's support from the house of Baal-berith."),
        judges(9, 1, "Judges", 8, 31, "Abimelech's claim on Shechem rests on his being Gideon's son by the concubine who lived there."),
        judges(9, 4, "Judges", 8, 33, "The house of Baal-berith that funds Abimelech grows out of Israel's post-Gideon turn to that god."),
        judges(9, 7, "Deuteronomy", 11, 29, "Jotham speaks from Mount Gerizim, the mountain Moses appointed for proclaiming covenant blessing in the land."),
        judges(9, 28, "Genesis", 34, 2, "Shechem's ruler descends from the city and family first introduced through Hamor and his son in Jacob's history there."),
        judges(9, 53, "2 Samuel", 11, 21, "Joab later invokes Abimelech's death by a woman's millstone as a warning about approaching a city wall."),
        judges(10, 6, "2 Kings", 23, 13, "Josiah later destroys high places devoted to several of the foreign gods whose worship had drawn Israel into rebellion."),
        judges(10, 10, "1 Samuel", 12, 10, "Israel's confession to Samuel repeats the same cycle of abandoning the Lord for the Baals and Ashtaroth and pleading for rescue."),
        judges(10, 11, "Exodus", 14, 30, "The Lord's reminder of deliverance from Egypt grounds his rebuke of Israel's repeated abandonment after rescue."),
